Q: write png quickly A: <p>It took me some time to implement this based on the answers already existing, including finding out the exact details of how some of the data is to be written or processed. Therefore I write this as a separate answer which combines the information in the answers, the comments, and other corners of the internet I needed to understand and implement the entire process from scratch. This includes a rough order of things, both some descriptions and pseudocode, and at the end, there's a live demo implementing the process in JavaScript and using it to generate and display a low-resolution pixel graphic.</p>
<p>Here is, step-by-step, how to create a basic, uncompressed PNG out of pixel data.</p>
<h2>Input data format</h2>
<p>This works with image data stored as an array of <em>height</em> size, storing rows of <em>width</em> pixels, each an integer representing a 24-bit colour (0x000000 through 0xFFFFFF); this was done mostly for convenience of displaying the data in the code of the demo I threw together in JavaScript, and only the code that reads that image data and converts it into scanline data (as PNG calls it) needs to be adjusted to work with any other representation (such as a simple array where one works with offsets and stride, which may be faster depending on the actual language and implementation).</p>
<p>For the sake of completeness, a big-endian integer as 4 bytes looks like it would be written in code, padded with zeros to be exactly 4 bytes; for example, the number 600 in decimal is 0x258, so it becomes 0x00000258, or the bytes 0x00, 0x00, 0x02, 0x58.</p>
<p>Before assembling the actual PNG file together, the image data needs to be turned into the proper format.</p>
<h2>Preparing image data</h2>
<p>First, turn the image data into PNG <em>scanline data</em>.</p>
<pre><code>
scanline data = []
for row in image data 
    add bytes [0] to scanline data
    add bytes [R, G, B] for each pixel in row
</code></pre>
<h2>DEFLATE</h2>
<p>Then, package the scanline data into a zlib/deflate format - this is also required for image data even if it's not going to be compressed.</p>
<p>Split the data into chunks of no more than 65535 bytes, there is no requirement to do full 65535 bytes except for the last chunk as far as I know, but it's the easiest most efficient. Each chunk starts with 5 bytes of header. For our purposes, the first byte is 1 for the last (or only) chunk, and 0 for others. The remaining 4 are chunk size, written in little-endian 16-bit (hence the size limit), and then again, but with all the bits flipped (bitwise XOR does wonders here).</p>
<pre><code>deflated data = []
remaining = length of scanline data
offset = 0

if remaining &gt; 65535 
    add bytes [0, 255, 255, 0, 0] to deflated data
    add 65535 bytes from scanline data, starting at offset, to deflated data
    advance offset by 65535
else
    add byte [1] to deflated data
    add byte [remaining &amp; 0xFF] to deflated data
    add byte [(remaining &amp; 0xFF00) &gt;&gt; 8] to deflated data
    add byte [(remaining &amp; 0xFF) XOR 255] to deflated data
    add byte [((remaining &amp; 0xFF00) &gt;&gt; 8) XOR 255] to deflated data
    add the rest of bytes from scanline data, starting at offset, to deflated data
    done
</code></pre>
<p>Finish it up by performing the Adler32 algorithm on scanline data, adding the 4 bytes of the result at the end of the deflated data.</p>
<h2>Chunks</h2>
<p>Now, the chunks can be created.</p>
<pre><code>IHDR = [73, 72, 68, 82]
    add image width as big-endian integer, 4 bytes, to IHDR
    add image height as big-endian integer, 4 bytes, to IHDR
    add bytes [8, 2, 0, 0, 0] to IHDR

IDAT = [73, 68, 65, 84]
    add bytes [120, 1] to IDAT
    add deflated data to IDAT

</code></pre>
<p>This chunk is always the last one and it is always identical: size of 0, &quot;IEND&quot;, CRC32 of &quot;IEND&quot;.</p>
<pre><code>IEND = [0, 0, 0, 0, 73, 69, 78, 68, 174, 66, 96, 130]
</code></pre>
<p>Now the PNG file can be written as all necessary data processing is done.</p>
<pre><code>write bytes [137, 80, 78, 71, 13, 10, 26, 10]
write IHDR size as big-endian integer, 4 bytes, (this is always [0, 0, 0, 13])
write IHDR
write CRC32 of IHDR
write IDAT size as big-endian integer, 4 bytes
write IDAT
write CRC32 of IHDR
write IEND
</code></pre>
<p>Below is a short demo implementing all of the above and displaying a simple pixel graphic. If you save the image, you will see that it is a true PNG file.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!doctype html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
&lt;style&gt;
body 
{
    background-color: #000;    
}
/*
    This fits the resulting image without stretching or cropping
    and keeps the pixels nice looking
*/
img 
{
    image-rendering: pixelated;
    image-rendering: -moz-crisp-edges;
    image-rendering: crisp-edges;
    width:100vmin;
    height: 100vmin;
}
&lt;/style&gt;
&lt;script&gt;


//////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//                                                  //
//                Algorithms                        //
//                                                  //
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////

// CRC32 LUT and algo
// got from here
// https://stackoverflow.com/a/18639999

var makeCRCTable = function(){
    var c;
    var crcTable = [];
    for(var n =0; n &lt; 256; n++){
        c = n;
        for(var k =0; k &lt; 8; k++){
            c = ((c&amp;1) ? (0xEDB88320 ^ (c &gt;&gt;&gt; 1)) : (c &gt;&gt;&gt; 1));
        }
        crcTable[n] = c;
    }
    return crcTable;
}
// original worked with strings, modified to work with the byte arrays
// returns as 4 bytes
var crc32 = function(arr) {
    var crcTable = window.crcTable || (window.crcTable = makeCRCTable());
    var crc = 0 ^ (-1);

    for (var i = 0; i &lt; arr.length; i++ ) {
        crc = (crc &gt;&gt;&gt; 8) ^ crcTable[(crc ^ arr[i]) &amp; 0xFF];
    }
    crc = (crc ^ (-1)) &gt;&gt;&gt; 0;
    return bigend(crc);
};


// naive implementation of adler32
// it is a checksum that is part of the "compressed" data
// unrelated to the CRC32 checksum used by PNG
// very simple algorithm, written given Wikipedia's explanation
function adler32(data)
{
    let A = 1;
    let B = 0;
    let L = data.length;
    for(let i = 0; i&lt;L; i++)
    {
        A += data[i];
        B += A;
    }
    A = A % 65521;
    B = B % 65521;
    // spec actually returns a 32-bit integer
    // but since it's just to be written anyway
    // might as well return the bytes
    return [B &gt;&gt;&gt; 8, B &amp; 0xFF, A &gt;&gt;&gt; 8, A &amp; 0xFF];
}


//////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//                                                  //
//                Utility                           //
//                                                  //
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////


// writes a 32-bit int as 4 big-endian bytes
function bigend(num)
{
    return [(num &amp; 0xFF000000) &gt;&gt;&gt; 24, (num &amp; 0xFF0000) &gt;&gt;&gt; 16, (num &amp; 0xFF00) &gt;&gt;&gt; 8, (num &amp; 0xFF)];
}

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//                                                  //
//                Data formatting                   //
//                                                  //
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////

// turns array of RGB values into a series of bytes
// this is for 24-bit images specifically (0x08 0x02 PNG type)
// implementation specific - image data is stored as integer values
// and gets unpacked into byte triplets
function rgb2scanline(line)
{
    let data = [];
    // literally RRGGBB =&gt; RR GG BB
    line.forEach((v)=&gt;{
        data.push((v &amp; 0xFF0000)&gt;&gt;&gt;16);
        data.push((v &amp; 0x00FF00)&gt;&gt;&gt;8);
        data.push((v &amp; 0x0000FF));
    });
    return data;
}
// turns image data into scanlines for PNG format
// outputs an array of bytes of scanline data
// exact access/writes are implementation specific
// using push and arrays here in JavaScript for convenience
function makescanlines(imagedata)
{
    let data = [];
    // this operates on imagedata being an array of 
    // pixel rows (which are an array of colour values each)
    imagedata.forEach((r)=&gt;{
        // prepend each row with a 0 
        // which means data as is
        data.push(0);
        // add the row turned into sequential bytes
        data.push(...rgb2scanline(r));
    });
    return data;
}
// turns the raw scanlines data into a "proper" deflated
// uncompressed chunk of data
// this is what goes into the IDAT
function makedeflate(scanlines)
{
    let data = [];
    let ptr = 0;
    let remaining = scanlines.length;
    // basically keep writing out blocks, at most
    // 65535 bytes at a time 
    // part of the block header explicitly specifies
    // that no compression is used
    while(remaining &gt; 0)
    {
        if(remaining &gt; 65535)
        {
            // fixed block header for any non-final block
            // as it is guaranteed to be 65535 bytes in size
            // in this implementation as that gives the least overhead
            data.push(0, 255, 255, 0, 0);
            // writeout 65535 bytes and advance
            data = data.concat(scanlines.slice(ptr, 65535));
            ptr += 65535;
            remaining-=65535;
        }
        else
        {
            // block size can be anything so needs to be written
            // size and ones complement, little endian
            data.push(
                1, // last block
                remaining &amp; 0xFF, 
                (remaining &amp; 0xFF00) &gt;&gt;&gt; 8, 
                (remaining &amp; 0xFF) ^ 0xFF, 
                ((remaining &amp; 0xFF00) &gt;&gt;&gt; 8) ^ 0xFF
            );
            // write all data that's left and done
            data = data.concat(scanlines.slice(ptr));
            remaining=0;
        }
    }
    // checksum required by the deflate/zlib container
    data.push(...adler32(scanlines));
    return data;
}

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//                                                  //
//                Showtime                          //
//                                                  //
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////



function makePNG(imagedata)
{
    let h = imagedata.length;
    let w = imagedata[0].length;
    // PNG signature (8 bytes), IHDR size, fixed
    let pngheader = [137, 80, 78, 71, 13, 10, 26, 10, 0, 0, 0, 13];
    // IHDR chunk id
    let IHDR = [73, 72, 68, 82, ...bigend(w), ...bigend(h), 8, 2, 0, 0, 0];
    // IEND - size, chunk ID, CRC, fixed
    let IEND = [0, 0, 0, 0, 73, 69, 78, 68, 174, 66, 96, 130];
    // each pixel is 3 bytes, one extra byte per scanline
    let rawimagesize = h * ((w * 3) + 1);
    // deflate chunk count
    let deflateblockcount = Math.ceil(rawimagesize/65535);
    // scanline data size + 2 bytes of header + 5 bytes per block header + 4 bytes adler32 checksum
    let deflatesize = rawimagesize + 2 + (5 * deflateblockcount) + 4;
    let totalsize = 
        pngheader.length + IHDR.length  // both the actual header and IDHR up to 
        4 + 4 +             // width and height
        5 +                 // rest of IHDR, fixed
        4 +                 // CRC of IHDR
        4 + 4 +             // IDAT size + chunk ID
        deflatesize +       // zlib header and deflate data
        4 +                 // CRC of IDAT
        IEND.length    // IEND 
        ;

    // this will be the "file" written to
    let PNG = [];
    // prepare the image data into scanlines
    let scanlinedata = makescanlines(imagedata);
    // pack into a zlib/DEFLATE format, this will be the data written
    let deflated = makedeflate(scanlinedata);

    // write header
    PNG = PNG.concat(pngheader);
    // get IHDR CRC - only width and height varies
    // can precompute for specific image sizes if needed
    let IHDR_crc = crc32(IHDR);
    IHDR.push(...IHDR_crc);
    // write IHDR
    PNG = PNG.concat(IHDR);
    // IDAT size
    PNG.push(...bigend(deflatesize));
    let IDAT = []; 
    // "IDAT"
    IDAT.push(73, 68, 65, 84);
    // zlib header 0x78 0x01 specifying no compression
    IDAT.push(120, 1);
    // add the data
    IDAT = IDAT.concat(deflated);
    // crc the whole thing and add to end
    let IDAT_crc = crc32(IDAT);
    IDAT.push(...IDAT_crc);
    // write IDAT
    PNG = PNG.concat(IDAT);
    // write IEND
    PNG = PNG.concat(IEND);

    return PNG;
}
&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
&lt;img id="target" /&gt;


&lt;script&gt;

// here's some pixels to test with
// each hex value is one pixel
// image is array of rows
// row is array of pixels

var imageData = [
    [0x000000, 0x000000,0xFFFFFF,0xFFFFFF,0xFFFFFF,0xFFFFFF,0x000000,0x000000],
    [0x000000, 0xFFFFFF,0x000000,0x000000,0x000000,0x000000,0xFFFFFF,0x000000],
    [0xFFFFFF, 0x000000,0xFF0000,0x000000,0x000000,0x00FF00,0x000000,0xFFFFFF],
    [0xFFFFFF, 0x000000,0xFF0000,0x000000,0x000000,0x00FF00,0x000000,0xFFFFFF],
    [0xFFFFFF, 0x000000,0x000000,0x000000,0x000000,0x000000,0x000000,0xFFFFFF],
    [0xFFFFFF, 0x000000,0xFF00FF,0xFF00FF,0xFF00FF,0xFF00FF,0x000000,0xFFFFFF],
    [0x000000, 0xFFFFFF,0x000000,0x000000,0x000000,0x000000,0xFFFFFF,0x000000],
    [0x000000, 0x000000,0xFFFFFF,0xFFFFFF,0xFFFFFF,0xFFFFFF,0x000000,0x000000]
];

// that's it

let myPNG = makePNG(imageData);

// could probably use one of these for other operations to make it faster
// but this is a demo anyway

let buf = new Uint8Array(myPNG);

// this is the actual reason
let b64 = buf.toBase64();

// display the image that's literally it
document.getElementById('target').src = "data:image/png;base64," + b64;

&lt;/script&gt;


&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: How to approximate Java&#39;s Character.isLetterOrDigit() to identify non-English letters, digits in Javascript? A: <p>These match non-Latin digits and letters too:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// Equivalant to Java Character.isDigit(c)
function isDigit(character) {
                                    // \p{digit} is equivalant to \p{Nd}
    return character?.length === 1 &amp;&amp; /\p{digit}/u.test(character);
    // return /^\p{digit}$/u.test(character); // This returns true if the argument is a number like 4 instead of &quot;4&quot; too
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// Equivalant to Java Character.isLetter(c)
function isLetter(character) {
    return character?.length === 1 &amp;&amp; /\p{L}/u.test(character);
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>isDigit(&quot;4&quot;); // True
isDigit(&quot;۴&quot;); // true (Persian)

isLetter(&quot;a&quot;); // True
isLetter(&quot;ش&quot;); // True (Persian)
</code></pre>
